  "text_md": "![](<2503.09133_pg5_images/imageFile1.png>)\n\n1\n\nFigure 1. This picture represents the image under VAL of a line tangent to the quadric Q ( K ).\n\na matrix power series A ( t ) with det( A ( t )) not identically zero power series. Normalize to have det( A ( t )) = 1. Then A ( t ) = t γ cB + o ( t γ ) with γ > 0 , and c ∈ C ∗ . Make change of variables in A ( t ) giving ˜ A ( t ) by replacing each t β by exp( − βγ − 1 σ ) t αβγ − 1 , where σ = log( c ). This comes from the necessity to obtain the image of the term ct γ in the desired form as t α , and it is an automorphism of K fixing C . Then ˜ A ( t ) = t α B + o ( t α ) ∈ V ( K ) ∩ PSL 2 ( K ) and VAL( ˜ A ( t )) = ( α, [ B ] R ∗ ). □\n\n# 3 ExAMPLES\n\nExample 1. We describe the image under VAL of a particular line L tangent to the quadric Q ( K ) . Consider L ⊂ K P 3 parametrized by\n\n$$\ntw PSL2 K = KP3 , t-1w\n$$\n\nt ∈ K being the uniformizer. The point where L intersects the quadric Q ( K ) is B ∞ =   0 1 0 0   corresponding to w = 0 .\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFor all other values of [ z : 1] ∈ K P 1 and z = ct γ + o ( t γ ) , c ∈ C ∗ , we have three cases: γ > 1, γ = 1 and γ < 1. Look for the limit at ∞ for z represented by decreasing exponents. 0 ct γ\n\nct In the first case; the dominant term of Z(t) is Projected by VAL an S1 in the circle bundle $.\n\nIn the second case, the dominant term is   0 0   , giving a section of the S bundle over Q ( C ) with points   1 0 0 0   and   0 1 0 0   excluded.\n\n",
